## 🧭 Workflow & verification guardrails

<MermaidDiagram
  chart={workflowChart}
  eyebrow="Verification flow"
  title="Verified AI-assisted engineering workflow"
/>

The point of this loop is not to let AI write code immediately. Every change must pass through **context gathering → scoped implementation → verification → review**. As the task grows, I care less about “what was generated” and more about “what evidence proves it is safe to ship.”

**Verification guardrails**

| Step | Standard | If it fails |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Context gathering | Read relevant files and existing patterns first | Stop guessing and inspect the source |
| Scope control | Touch only files directly tied to the request | Split unrelated diffs before commit |
| Static validation | Pass `pnpm check` and `pnpm lint` | Fix from logs, not blind retries |
| Content validation | Keep localized detail, alt, caption, and links in sync | Block one-locale-only changes |
| Browser validation | Verify the rendered local page | Rework from DOM/screenshot evidence |
| Commit review | Separate logical diff groups | Split mixed concerns before commit |

**Concrete examples**

- **Metric cleanup:** Removed years, technology names, and status labels from metric cards, keeping only values that represent outcome, scale, or measurable improvement.
- **Tech-stack pruning:** Removed weak signals such as `Lottie`, `CVA`, and generic AI tools from project stacks, keeping AI usage represented only in the workflow card.
- **Browser-based UI verification:** Checked responsive metric layouts, project-detail table styling, and homepage visibility directly in the local browser before marking work complete.
- **Evidence hygiene:** Removed screenshots that looked AI-generated and avoided using images as portfolio evidence unless they came from real product or reproducible local output.

## 💭 Retrospective

**Focus shift to design and validation.** AI assistance is useful when the engineer still owns architecture, tradeoffs, review, and verification. The workflow is designed to make that ownership explicit.

**Exponential development velocity.** Complex refactors and boilerplate tasks that used to take days now take a single session. Product launch cycles shorten in proportion to how well the directive infrastructure is maintained.

**Consistent quality floor.** Agents enforce conventions and type checks at the infrastructure level that humans routinely miss under time pressure — code quality becomes a baseline, not a target to aim at.
